// A call is a *construction* only when the callee spelling names the
// class. monomorphize asked instead whether the call's inferred type --
// which for an ordinary call is simply its return type -- named a known
// class, and if so treated the call's own arguments as that class's
// constructor arguments. `describe(3, 4)` returning a `Boxed` therefore
// became `Boxed{3, 4}`: a two-argument construction of a class whose
// constructor takes one.
//
// That was inert only while no such class had a constructor *template*,
// since the instantiation loop then found nothing to build. With one,
// the bogus construction deduced the template parameter from an
// unrelated argument and emitted a clone whose body could not be
// checked at all -- which is how it first showed up, as
// libs/scpp/rand/scpp_rand.scpp's `uniform_int_distribution<int>::make`
// failing inside std::expected's constructor once that constructor
// became a template.
import std;

class Boxed {
public:
    std::string text{};
    template<typename U>
    Boxed(U source) { text = std::string(source); }
    virtual ~Boxed() = default;
};

Boxed describe(int width, int height) {
    Boxed result{"box"};
    return result;
}

int main() { return static_cast<int>(describe(3, 4).text.length()); }
